#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153433 :tutorial/content "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are **disjoint** if they have no element in common. In other words, their intersection is empty: $$A \\cap B = \\emptyset$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$A = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 5,10 \\}$$\n\nThese sets are disjoint since their intersection is empty:\n\n$$A\\cap B = \\{ -1,-2,-3 \\} \\cap \\{ 5,10 \\} = \\emptyset$$\n\nOn the other hand, $C=(-\\infty,5]$ and $D=(-2,\\infty)$ are *not* disjoint since the intersection of these sets is *not* empty: $$C\\cap D = (-\\infty,5] \\cap (-2,\\infty) = (-2,5] \\neq \\emptyset$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92860310-1be6-50cf-8447-2b24a95db5e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153434 :tutorial/content "The **disjoint union** of two sets is defined differently depending on whether the sets are non-overlapping (disjoint) or overlapping (have a non-empty intersection). We'll use the special symbol \"$\\sqcup$\" (square cup) to denote the disjoint union.\n\nThe disjoint union of two non-overlapping (disjoint) sets $A$ and $B$ is simply the union of the sets.\n\nFor example, since\n\n$$\\{ 0,2,4 \\} \\cap \\{ 1, 3 \\} = \\emptyset,$$\n\nwe find the disjoint union of these sets as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{0,2,4}⊔{1,3} & ={0,2,4}\\cup {1,3} \\\\ & ={0,1,2,3,4}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d264ae6-080d-5ca0-be06-b6d41a5f4aa7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8005 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153436 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the following overlapping sets:\n\n$$A_1 = \\{ \\square, \\triangle \\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{ \\heartsuit, \\square\\}.$$\n\nThese sets overlap because their intersection is non-empty. $$A_1 \\cap A_2 = \\{ \\square \\} \\neq \\emptyset.$$ To construct the disjoint union of these overlapping sets, we proceed as follows:\n\n- For each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is \"tagged\" with the corresponding index: $$\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} & & \\,\\to \\, & & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(◻,1),(△,1)} \\\\ & {A}_{2} & & \\,\\to \\, & & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(♡,2),(◻,2)}\\end{aligned}$$\n- As a result, the new sets $A_1^\\ast$ and $A_2^\\ast$ are non-overlapping. So, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets: $$\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={◻,△}⊔{♡,◻} \\\\ & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\ & ={(◻,1),(△,1)}\\cup {(♡,2),(◻,2)} \\\\ & ={(◻,1),(△,1),(♡,2),(◻,2)}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e3b30b0-95ac-5b38-a7e6-169cc3409776" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8137 :tutorial/title "The Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"}]}
